title: The Psalms of David's Flight, Part 1 – The Flight Begins
description: The Psalms of David's Flight, Part 1 of 3: Michal lets David down through the window (Psalm 59, 1 Samuel 19), and David feigns madness at Gath (Psalms 56 and 34, 1 Samuel 21), the two Psalm studies read in full, in the King James Bible.
output: lessons/bible/davids-flight-1-the-flight-begins.html

# The Psalms of David's Flight, part 1 of 3 (a Wednesday-night lesson, read aloud from a laptop, about 30-35 minutes).
# Built from, in full and in their own order:
#   Psalm 59 study (review/refile.json drive "psalms-59"; as in the app, Psalms.json -> studies -> "59")
#   Psalm 56 study, with its Psalm 34 part (drive "psalms-56"; Psalms.json -> studies -> "56")
# Layout (this series, three parts):
#   title slide (overline "The Psalms of David's Flight · Part N of 3", heading, sub of the psalms, refs; Part 2 adds
#   note: Optional), then "Where we are" (step: one line per part, the current one marked "tonight").
#   Each study opens with the psalm read in full (scripture:, in two or three slides), then the study paragraph by
#   paragraph in its own order and wording, one or two paragraphs to a slide; every passage the study quotes follows
#   the sentence that introduces it, as scripture: (big: for one verse), so the reference is always on the slide.
#   Kickers name the psalm or the 1 Samuel chapter and the place: "Psalm 59 · The window", "1 Samuel 19 · The window".
#   Between two studies a transition slide carries only a heading naming the next passage.
#   The part ends with each study's own closing paragraphs, then "Next time" (or "Read on") with read: links.
# Verses the studies typed out are shown from the KJV by the builder. Words of a verse that sit inside a sentence of the
# study (for example *The God of my mercy shall prevent me*) stay in the sentence as typed.
# The Psalm 34 heading "A Psalm of David, when he changed his behaviour before Abimelech; who drove him away, and he
# departed." is quoted by the study and is kept as typed (the KJV title of the psalm, not a numbered verse).
# Fixed: "an a host" -> "and a host" was already fixed in the app's text; "(Luke 22:43)" and "(Philippians 2:5)" after
# words quoted in a sentence are shown as verse buttons. Nothing else needed a fix.

