=== The window · Psalm 59
--- Psalm 59
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
scripture: Psalm 59:1-6

--- Psalm 59, continued
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
scripture: Psalm 59:7-12

--- Psalm 59, continued
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
scripture: Psalm 59:13-17

--- Michal let David down through a window
kicker: 1 Samuel 19 · The window
p: We often forget the flights of David were the impetus of so many Psalms. Psalm 59 was written as Saul, David's King and father in law sent men to David's house to capture and kill him, while Michal, David's wife and daughter of the King aided in his escape, being lowered from the window to flee.
scripture: 1 Samuel 19:10-12 | mark: So Michal let David down through a window

--- Michal let David down through a window, continued
kicker: 1 Samuel 19 · The window
scripture: 1 Samuel 19:13-18

--- A harrowing time
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: A harrowing time, likely more than a decade where the future King lives as a fugitive, hunted, lied about and sought by wicked men in King Saul's service.
p: And we see that David's faithfulness towards God as His deliverer was present from the beginning.

--- Without fault
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: He trusts God to deliver him from the bloody men coming round his home, barking like dogs, saying cruel lies.
p: David is innocent of wrongdoing, yet they seek his life. Without fault, pursued unto death, he cries out for the Lord to awake and help him.

--- The prayer of Jesus
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: This is similar to the prayer of Jesus, the sinless Son of God, in the hours leading up to His arrest.
big: Matthew 26:39

--- The joy set before Him
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: Like David, Jesus was sought by workers of iniquity, bloody men. Falsely accused, and yet knowing the joy set before Him, endured.
big: Hebrews 12:2

--- An angel from heaven
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: Jesus, knowing what was set before Him, prayed.
big: Luke 22:43

--- Twelve legions of angels
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: He, like David, could have asked for deliverance, and a host of angels to come and save Him.
big: Matthew 26:53

--- Obedient unto death
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: But for the sake of the cross, Jesus allowed them to take Him.
big: Philippians 2:8

--- Able to deliver
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: However, the willingness of Christ to be obedient unto death doesn't change the fact of Almighty God being able to deliver in a time of persecution.
p: David being an image of the persecuted Son, being sinless in the matter with which he was accused, not sinless himself, shows the general application of deliverance when we are wrongly persecuted.

--- Let this mind be in you
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: If we *Let this mind be in you, which was also in Christ Jesus* [[Philippians 2:5]], and have a willingness to suffer tribulation, while trusting on the Lord, like David, to protect us, we will not be ashamed.
scripture: 2 Timothy 2:11-12

--- Let this mind be in you, continued
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
scripture: 2 Timothy 3:12; 1 Peter 4:16

--- A way to escape
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: God is able to deliver us;
big: 1 Corinthians 10:13

--- The mind of Christ
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: David had the mind of Christ. He glorified God in his temptations and trials. He counted God's mercy and judgement as something to grasp, to know for sure that He is, and a deliverer of those that call upon Him.
scripture: Psalm 59:10; Psalm 59:17 | mark: The God of my mercy shall prevent me

--- A man after God's own heart
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: David continues in tribulation for years, maintaining this outlook, as witnessed across the Psalm, from this initial trial, through the time in the wilderness, until God rewarded him with his crown. A man after God's own heart.
p: The late evening setting of Psalm 59, as the men dispatched by Saul come for David, mirrors the midnight garden setting when the high priests men came for Jesus.

--- David took the window
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: David knew he would be delivered because he believed God, he knew the line that would eventually produce the incarnation of Christ was promised, the eternal Kingdom. And while Jesus rejected the hosts to save Him, David took the window, the way God made for his escape.

--- All things for good
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: The betrayal of Saul matches the betrayal of Judas, trusted men turning on the blameless, in service of evil.
p: But God uses all things for good.

--- The God of my mercy shall prevent me
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: When David writes, *The God of my mercy shall prevent me*, he isn't using the modern understanding of the word, to stop or hinder, rather the actual definition of the word; to "go before" or "anticipate." His faith is that the mercy of God would be there with him, providing that means of escape. Knowing that the Lord would not leave him ashamed.
p: The mercy of God has already mapped out the boundary of the trial, and the window exit would be the means God had already allowed.

--- In the morning
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: And, the night past, the fleeing David sings of the mercy of God in the morning, even as the dogs are barking around his door.
p: Just as David found deliverance in the morning, Christ’s voluntary obedience unto death led to the ultimate morning of the resurrection.

--- Hope maketh not ashamed
kicker: Psalm 59 · The window
p: Because He did not take the window, He broke the power of death entirely, ensuring that for every believer facing tribulation, hope maketh not ashamed;
big: Romans 5:5
center: Amen

=== Gath · Psalms 56 and 34
--- 1 Samuel 21 · Gath [title]
heading: 1 Samuel 21 · Gath

--- Psalm 56
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
scripture: Psalm 56:1-7

--- Psalm 56, continued
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
scripture: Psalm 56:8-13

--- When I am afraid
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: Psalm 56 was written commemorating the flight of David from Saul, when it seemed all Israel was against him and sought David's life.
p: He prays for mercy from the Lord, in the midst of this tribulation, reminding himself that, when he is afraid, he will trust in God.

--- A life-verse
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: For some of us, *What time I am afraid, I will trust in thee* could be a life-verse.
p: Being fearful is a sin. Having fear itself is not. This is an important distinction that we should constantly remind ourselves of. We see David throughout the Psalms express fear. He is a mighty man of valor, great in battle, a giant-killer, a man of great faith, and yet he feels fear.

--- Fear and being fearful
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: Fear is a natural response to the threats against us and those that we love. Fear is something God has given us in order to react to immediate stimuli and avoid danger. To elevate our awareness in situations where we have to be on guard. Fear saves lives.
p: But being fearful, the condition where we take that warning system, and become slaves to it, where we allow present troubles of the world and being careful for everything, leave us unable to move, or to see the power of God in our lives.

--- Despair comes from fearfulness
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: Despair comes from fearfulness. Sin is at the door of those trapped in it. We are at our worst when we allow the stress of fear to blind us, bind us and send us into spiraling worry, where we begin to feel fear for things that haven't even presented themselves. For future events we have only seen in our vain imagination. When rather than hope in God that all things will work for good, we see only evil and destruction.

--- Be not afraid
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: Our God is bigger than all the cares and tribulations of this life. And fearing only God trumps anything else we may be afraid of.
p: The myth that the KJV has 365 verses saying not to fear isn't true but there are many, many times, where God tells us to be not afraid, for He is with us. The Bible uses variations of "fear not" or "be not afraid" approximately 100 to 150 times, with the broader theme of finding courage because God is with us appearing over 300 times throughout the entire Scripture.

--- Fear is a test of faith
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: Fear is a test of faith. Not a test of salvation, it takes an incredibly tiny amount of trust to believe God and accept Christ, and we are not saying that being afraid should shake that trust or make you question your eternal life. David, the apple of God's eye, felt fear. Again, it is a natural reaction to danger.

--- To have peace and rest in the Lord
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: But the Christian walk is not about finding enough faith to trust Jesus to save you, but about building upon that foundation a progressive reliance on God to handle everything in that life, that we may live it more abundantly. To have peace and rest in the Lord.
p: And so, fear is a test of faith. Does it make you fearful, and bring you low, to react in sin, or does it humble you to giving that dread up to be handled by Him, and have confidence in that very trial that God will see you through?

--- He fears less
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: Fear is an opportunity to increase your faith and bring your feet to stay in His ways, to walk His paths.
p: The righteous man isn't fearless, he fears less. Casting all his cares upon God, trusting in His deliverance. His judgments. Knowing that whatever may come, He has you in His hands.
big: Isaiah 41:10

--- Fear only God
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: We should only fear God. Let Him handle the rest.
big: Matthew 10:28

--- Fear not, for I am with thee
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: God tells us again and again Fear not, for I am with thee. David confesses this truth again and again, and places his safety and security entirely upon the Lord.
big: Psalm 56:4

--- Threatened daily
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: David is threatened daily, by enemies and betrayers within the Kingdom, and without. By Saul and his armies, turncoats and opportunistic men. Hearing lies and rumors repeated against him, spread by the wicked to poison opinions against him.

--- At the gates of Gath
kicker: 1 Samuel 21 · Gath
p: Here in Psalm 56, David has been delivered to the Kingdom of Gath by his flight. He stood at the gates, to seek an audience with King Achish. But, he notices the words of the guards, and the heightened senses sparked by fear cause him to sense potential trouble.
scripture: 1 Samuel 21:10-13 | mark: was sore afraid of Achish; feigned himself mad

--- Lo, ye see the man is mad
kicker: 1 Samuel 21 · Gath
p: In this case fear helped David to react quickly. Acting like a madman when he hears the guards recognize who he is.
scripture: 1 Samuel 21:14-15

--- He seeks comfort in the Lord
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: Rather than being taken captive and potentially turned over to Saul, David's feigned madness allows him to escape and continue his flight. His short-term fear protected him in a harrowing situation.
p: However, long-term, he seeks comfort in the Lord.

--- Continuing in Psalm 56
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: He prays the enemies be cast down. To be shown God's strength and wrath. To turn the tables of the fear they may bring out in David, and instead fill them with fear of his God.
p: Continuing in Psalm 56.

--- Put thou my tears into thy bottle
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: David writes of his trust in the faithfulness of God, in the face of his fears;
scripture: Psalm 56:8-9 | mark: put thou my tears into thy bottle

--- The very hairs of your head
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: God loves His children, and knows our pain and struggles. He savors our prayer and captures our tears and numbers every step that we take, that He may direct our paths.
p: Jesus says the same, speaking directly to us;
big: Luke 12:7

--- A Savior who understands
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: The Lord knows our troubles and hears us in adversity. We have a Savior, Who the Bible says, understands what we go through.
big: Hebrews 4:15

--- I will render praises unto thee
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: He will never leave us or forsake us. We who praise His name and lean on His mercy, will never be ashamed.
p: David writes:
scripture: Psalm 56:10; Psalm 56:12

--- Confess me before men
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: God will deliver us that we may sing His praises. Confessing Jesus as the author and finisher of our salvation.
big: Matthew 10:32

--- Who shall we fear?
kicker: Psalm 56 · Gath
p: The excellency of Christ, the power of God unto salvation, the right judge, full of righteousness and holy. Who shall we fear? What can befall us? If God be with us, who can stand against us? If we've been saved from the pit of Hell, delivered from the judgment and graciously given eternal life to praise His name, who shall we fear but God?
big: Psalm 56:13
center: Amen!

--- He delivered me from all my fears
kicker: Psalm 34 · Gath
p: David's confidence at the time of fear, fleeing from Saul and finding himself being challenged at the gates of Gath led to his deliverance. Psalm 34 tells of this. It is a complement to Psalm 56. The Bible says it is;
p: *A Psalm of David, when he changed his behaviour before Abimelech; who drove him away, and he departed.*
scripture: Psalm 34:1-4 | mark: I sought the LORD, and he heard me

--- O taste and see
kicker: Psalm 34 · Gath
p: David credits the Lord with hearing and answering his cry. The discernment to recognize the threat and allowing David's act to be sufficient for the King to allow him to continue his flight from Saul, results in being able to live another day. And for this, David gives joyful thanksgiving.
p: The confidence of [[Psalm 56:13]] ends in the song of worship, praising His name in Psalm 34.
big: Psalm 34:8

--- Give thanks in the congregation
kicker: Psalm 34 · Gath
p: Give thanks in the congregation when God has moved in your life. Praise His holy name among the people. Teach others to fear the Lord. Tell them of the works He has done in your life.
big: Psalm 34:18

--- Our fear leads to faith
kicker: Psalm 34 · Gath
p: Our fear leads to faith, by casting our cares upon the Lord. Our faith leads to proclaiming the truth of Christ, teaching men to fear only God. His mercy enduring forever.
p: It is the way. The truth. The light.

--- This is our purpose
kicker: Psalm 34 · Gath
p: Walking in the paths of righteousness. Calling upon Him when we are troubled or afraid. Being delivered. Proclaiming the truth of our God, confessing Jesus before men. Letting our light so shine that men may praise God in Heaven.
p: This is our purpose. This is why we suffer, so that men may praise Him. This is why we are blessed, so that men may praise Him.
center: Praise God for His lovingkindness and His mercy.

